  <hr />
  <p>If you’ve done Fourier series approximations of functions, you had three reactions to <a href="https://xkcd.com/">today’s XKCD comic</a>:</p>
<p><img alt="https://imgs.xkcd.com/comics/gravitational_square_wave.png" class="ss" src="https://imgs.xkcd.com/comics/gravitational_square_wave.png" title="https://imgs.xkcd.com/comics/gravitational_square_wave.png" width="100%"/></p>
<p>First, of course, was a smile of recognition. Then came a “wait a minute” as you realized that the <a href="https://leancrew.com/all-this/2023/02/swings-and-roundabouts/">Gibbs phenomenon</a> doesn’t get smaller as you add more terms to the Fourier expansion; it just concentrates itself at the corners:</p>
<p><img alt="Gibbs phenomenon examples for square wave" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2023/20230213-Gibbs%20phenomenon%20examples%20for%20square%20wave.png" title="Gibbs phenomenon examples for square wave" width="100%"/></p>
<p>That ringing never goes away. Here, for example, is the plot for</p>
<p><!-- $$ \sum_{k = 1, 3, 5, \ldots}^n \frac{4}{k \pi} \sin (k \pi x) $$ -->
<math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><mn>99</mn></munderover><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form="postfix" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></p>
<p><img alt="Square wave approximation" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261029-Square%20wave%20approximation.png" title="Square wave approximation" width="80%"/></p>
<p>Finally, you realized that thinking like this ruins the joke.</p>
  
